 "notes": [
  "MuJoCo path: C-level mujoco.rollout.Rollout(nthread=N) (persistent C++ thread pool), one MjData per thread, batched initial states (reset pose + N(0,0.1) qvel noise), open-loop uniform[-1,1] controls held for 8 substeps (np.repeat), preallocated state output (mjSTATE_FULLPHYSICS, 19 doubles/step). Rate = nbatch*ctrl_steps / wall time of one rollout call; median of 3 calls per config after warmup. Swept nthread in {1,5,11} x nbatch in {1024,4096,16384} x horizon {25,50,125} control steps; best config median reported.",
  "mujoco.rollout has native threading in this version (4.0.1, not 3.4.1 as assumed), so no separate threaded mj_step loop was needed; rollout already is a tight C mj_step loop.",
  "Ours re-measured in the same session with scripts/bench.py bench_mlx('walker', N, seconds=10) (same method as mujoco_walker_bench.py). Came in lower than the earlier 3.85M/7.61M because load average was ~12.6 at the start (machine busy with other processes); MuJoCo CPU numbers are also depressed by that load.",
  "Favourable to MuJoCo: no obs/reward/done computation, no auto-reset logic, no DC-motor speed-curve on ctrl (open loop), no Python per step. Ours includes GPU action sampling + obs/reward/done + auto-reset each step.",
  "Unfavourable to MuJoCo: no resets, so ~77-97% of walkers have fallen by rollout end and lie on the floor (likely more contacts than a standing walker); rollout also writes the full state trajectory to memory.",
  "Python mj_step loop (previous bench): 126.4K env-steps/s on 11 procs, 26.1K on 1 proc. C rollout: ~29K on 1 thread, ~150-165K on 11 threads, i.e. only ~1.3x better than the Python multi-process loop for this small model (per-step physics dominates, not Python overhead)."
